## Diagnosing Memory Leak — Step by Step

```bash
# STEP 1: Confirm it's a leak (not just working set)
# Monitor heap with JVM metrics over 24 hours:
# jvm_memory_used_bytes{area="heap"} should plateau after GC
# If it grows continuously → leak

# STEP 2: Enable heap OOM dump automatically:
java -XX:+HeapDumpOnOutOfMemoryError \
     -XX:HeapDumpPath=/var/log/app/heap.hprof \
     -jar myapp.jar
# Automatically captures heap dump when OOM occurs

# STEP 3: Trigger heap dump on running app:
jcmd <pid> GC.heap_dump /tmp/heap-$(date +%s).hprof
# OR:
jmap -dump:format=b,file=/tmp/heap.hprof <pid>
# OR via Spring Boot Actuator:
curl -o heap.hprof http://localhost:8080/actuator/heapdump

# STEP 4: Analyze with Eclipse MAT
# MAT > File > Open Heap Dump > heap.hprof
# Run: Leak Suspects Report
# Shows: "Problem 1: 850 instances of com.example.OrderCache,
#         total retained: 2.4GB (87% of heap)"
```

## Common Memory Leak Patterns

```java
// LEAK 1: Static collection growing without bound
public class MetricsCollector {
    // WRONG: Static map grows forever (never cleaned)
    private static Map<String, List<Long>> metrics = new HashMap<>();

    public void record(String metricName, long value) {
        metrics.computeIfAbsent(metricName, k -> new ArrayList<>()).add(value);
        // 1 million requests × avg 1KB per metric = 1GB over time
    }

    // FIX: Use bounded cache
    private static final Map<String, Long> metrics =
        Caffeine.newBuilder()
            .maximumSize(1000)
            .expireAfterWrite(Duration.ofMinutes(5))
            .build()
            .asMap();
}

// LEAK 2: Event listeners never removed
public class OrderProcessor implements ApplicationListener<OrderEvent> {
    // WRONG: Spring adds this as a singleton listener — fine
    // But if you manually add listeners:
    public void setup() {
        eventBus.addListener(event -> process(event));  // Lambda holds reference
        // Lambda is held by eventBus → OrderProcessor can't be GC'd
        // If OrderProcessor is created many times (e.g., per-request) → leak
    }

    // FIX: Remove listener when done
    public void teardown() {
        eventBus.removeListener(this);  // Or store listener reference and remove
    }
}

// LEAK 3: ThreadLocal not cleaned up
public class SecurityContextFilter implements Filter {
    private static final ThreadLocal<User> currentUser = new ThreadLocal<>();

    // WRONG:
    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse resp, FilterChain chain) {
        currentUser.set(extractUser(req));  // Set ThreadLocal
        chain.doFilter(req, resp);
        // If exception occurs or forgot: currentUser never cleared!
        // Thread goes back to pool WITH stale user reference
    }

    // FIX: Always clean up ThreadLocal:
    @Override
    public void doFilter(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse resp, FilterChain chain) {
        try {
            currentUser.set(extractUser(req));
            chain.doFilter(req, resp);
        } finally {
            currentUser.remove();  // ALWAYS clean up
        }
    }
}

// LEAK 4: Inner class capturing outer class reference
public class OrderService {
    private final List<Order> pendingOrders = new ArrayList<>();

    public Runnable createWorker() {
        // Anonymous class captures implicit reference to OrderService.this:
        return new Runnable() {
            @Override
            public void run() {
                // Uses pendingOrders (implicit OrderService.this.pendingOrders)
                pendingOrders.stream()...process...;
            }
        };
    }
    // If this Runnable is submitted to an executor and stays queued long:
    // → ExecutorService holds Runnable → Runnable holds OrderService
    // → OrderService can't be GC'd

    // FIX: Use static nested class or pass only what you need:
    public Runnable createWorker() {
        List<Order> ordersCopy = new ArrayList<>(pendingOrders);
        return () -> ordersCopy.stream()...process...;
        // Lambda captures ordersCopy, not the entire OrderService
    }
}
```

## Monitoring Memory in Production

```yaml
# Prometheus + Micrometer: track these metrics

jvm_memory_used_bytes{area="heap"}
  → Should plateau after GC cycles
  → Alert if trending up over 6 hours

jvm_memory_max_bytes{area="heap"}
  → Your -Xmx setting

jvm_gc_pause_seconds_sum / jvm_gc_pause_seconds_count
  → Average GC pause duration
  → Alert if > 200ms average

jvm_gc_memory_promoted_bytes_total
  → Rate of promotion from Young → Old Gen
  → High rate = objects living too long = potential leak or cache issue

jvm_gc_live_data_size_bytes
  → Size of Old Gen after GC
  → Should be relatively stable
  → If it grows over days → leak
```

## Production Reality

The `Caffeine` cache library solves 80% of memory leak issues from unbounded caches — always prefer `Caffeine.newBuilder().maximumSize(N).expireAfterWrite(Duration)` over `new HashMap()` for any long-lived cache. For ThreadLocal, the `try/finally` cleanup pattern is non-negotiable in thread pool environments (Tomcat, virtual threads). Heap dumps can be large (multiple GB) — take them during low-traffic windows and have enough disk space. Eclipse MAT's "Leak Suspects" report is remarkably accurate and usually identifies the problem in minutes.
